{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "1: Working with Tenants",
  "steps": [
    {
      "file": "",
      "line": 1,
      "description": "## Here's what you'll do:\n\n1.  Navigate through your default tenant and familiarize yourself with tenant settings\n1.  Learn about tenant environment tags and how they support the software development lifecycle (SDLC)\n1.  Locate documentation and support resources from your tenant",
      "title": "What you'll do"
    },
    {
      "file": "",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Explore the Getting Started page",
      "description": "## Explore the Getting Started page\n\n1. In a new browser tab, go to https://manage.auth0.com and ensure your default tenant is selected.\n      \n    When you access a brand new tenant, you are first presented with the **Getting Started** page. Take a moment to explore the contents of this page. In the Auth0 labs, we walk you through many of the same tasks and developer tools.\n1. To hide the Getting Started page on subsequent logins, scroll to the bottom of this page and select the **Hide this** link.\n    \n    ![select hide this](https://cdn.auth0.com/website/a0fun/v2/tenants-3.png)    \n    \n    > You will land on the Activity dashboard page on subsequent logins. You can always find the Getting Started page again by selecting your profile picture on the top right and then selecting **Getting Started**.\n",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 3,
          "character": 4
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 3,
          "character": 37
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "file": "",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Explore your default tenant's settings  ",
      "description": "## Explore your default tenant's settings  \n\n1. In the upper left-hand corner of the Dashboard, you’ll see your default tenant's name (starts with *dev-*).\n    You'll also see your tenant's location and environment tag. Environment tags are used to differentiate between development, staging, and production environments, which supports the software development lifecycle (SDLC). The default tenant is tagged as a **DEVELOPMENT** environment. We'll explore what this means in a moment.\n    \n    ![default tenant env tag](https://cdn.auth0.com/website/a0fun/v2/env-tag.png)  \n\n    \n1. Select the your tenant name to view the drop down menu with tenant control options.\n\n1. Select **Settings** from this menu to view the Tenants Settings page. You'll be on the **General** tab by default.\n\n1. Scroll down the Tenant Settings page to the **Environment Tag** section. You should see:\n    * Staging\n    * Development\n    * Production\n    >You can change the environment tag of your tenant here. For the purposes of our labs, we will be working in a development environment. \n\n1. Scroll back up and browse through the other tabs on the Tenants Settings page to familiarize yourself with some of the other settings.\n\n\n",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 1,
          "character": 4
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 1,
          "character": 44
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Give your tenant a friendly name",
      "description": "## Give your tenant a friendly name\n\n1. Return to the **General** tab for Tenant Settings.\n1. In the **Friendly Name** field, enter a name of your choice for this tenant.  \n1. Select **Save**.\n    This friendly name will show only for you when logged into the dashboard, and is meant to help you identify which tenant you're working in.  "
    },
    {
      "file": "",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "View users",
      "description": "## View users\n\nAuth0's cloud-hosted database stores information about your users accessible to you.  \n\n1. From the Auth0 dashboard, select **User Management** > **Users**. \n    > Notice that there are no users listed! You might expect to find your own user account listed here; however, this page only displays **end-users** and you are an administrative **tenant member**.\n\n1. From the Auth0 dashboard, select **Settings** > **Tenant Members**.   \n    > Since your account is a tenant member, you will see it listed here as an Admin.\n\n1. Select the three dots next to your name and then **Edit Roles** to view the different roles a tenant member can have. Notice that more granular roles require a paid subscription.\n\n1. Select your profile picture from the top right of the screen and select **Your profile**. Here you can explore and change your personal settings for this tenant. You can also view your current login sessions."
    },
    {
      "file": "",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Explore extensions",
      "description": "## Explore extensions\n\nAuth0 Extensions enable you to install applications or run commands/scripts that extend the functionality of the Auth0 base product.\n\nEach extension is separate from all other extensions. Auth0 defines extensions per tenant, so data is stored by the pair tenant\\\\extension.  \n\n1.  From the Auth0 dashboard menu, select **Extensions**.\n    The All Extensions tab is shown.   \n    Here, you can browse for and install a variety of official and 3rd party extensions on the tenant.  \n    \n2.  Explore the extensions under **All Extensions**. There's a variety of first- and third-party extensions available. \n\n\n3. Select the **Installed Extensions** tab; this will be empty since your tenant is new."
    },
    {
      "file": "",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Search and view logs",
      "description": "## Search and view logs\n\nAuth0 provides event logs that you can analyze to identify event anomalies.  \n\n1.  From the Auth0 dashboard, select **Monitoring** > **Logs**.  \n    > The Logs page lists actions taken in the dashboard by the administrators as well as authentications made by your end-users.  \n\n1. Locate the **API Operation** with a description of **Update tenant settings**. This log entry exists because you changed the Friendly Name of your tenant earlier in this lab.\n\n1.  Select **API Operation** to display the log entry and select **Open Details** to view it full screen.\n    - The **Summary** section will state when the event occurred and other metadata about the event. \n    - The **Details** section shows the log event data in JSON format.   \n    \n1.  Press CMD+F (Mac) or CTRL+F (PC) and search for **friendly_name** in the JSON. You'll see the entry updated with the name you provided earlier in the lab.\n"
    },
    {
      "file": "",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Locate documentation and support",
      "description": "## Locate documentation and support\n\nDocumentation, help, and support for Auth0 can be accessed from the dashboard.  \n\n1.  From the top menu, select **Documentation** > **Documentation** to access the Auth0 Documentation website in a new tab. You can close this tab when you are done exploring.\n\n1.  Now select **Documentation** > **API Explorers** to open the Auth0 APIs page.\n\n1. From this page, select **Management API** to reveal its available endpoints.\n    > Most of what you can do in the dashboard can also be done programmatically through the Management API.\n\n1.  Select **Management API** again to collapse the entry.\n\n1. Select **Authentication API** to reveal its endpoints. \n    > The Authentication API enables you to manage all aspects of user identity when you use Auth0. It offers endpoints so your users can log in, sign up, log out, access APIs, and more. Most developers won't work with this API directly but will rather use one of Auth0's [SDK libraries](https://auth0.com/docs/libraries).\n\n1.  Close this browser tab and return to your Auth0 dashboard.   \n1.  In the lower section of the left-hand navigation, select **Get Support** to go to the Auth0 Support Center page.   \n    >[support.auth0.com](https://support.auth0.com/) is where you can get support from the community or open a ticket.\n\n"
    },
    {
      "file": "",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "(Informational) Creating Additional Tenants",
      "description": "## (Informational) Creating Additional Tenants\n\nAs of October 31, 2023, free Auth0 accounts are limited to one tenant. \n\nHowever, if you have a paid Auth0 subscription, you will likely want to create additional tenants (i.e., staging and production) to support your software development lifecycle. This section walks you through creating additional tenants for your account. For information on how to achieve this, visit our documentation page on [Creating Multiple Tenants](https://auth0.com/docs/get-started/auth0-overview/create-tenants/create-multiple-tenants). "
    },
    {
      "file": "",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Recap",
      "description": "## Recap\n\nIn this lab, you:\n\n*   Explored your default *dev-* tenant, explored its settings, and updated the friendly name.\n*   Identified the types of environment tags and their purposes.\n*   Located where your end-users and tenant users are listed.\n*   Located where tenant extensions are listed.\n*   Located where log events are listed and inspected the log event generated when you changed your tenant’s friendly name.\n*   Located pages for Auth0 Documentation, APIs and SDK libraries, and Support Center.\n*   Identified how to create multiple tenants to support the Software Development Lifecycle (SDLC).\n"
    }
  ]
}